            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of 12-lead and computer-analysed 3 orthogonal lead electocardiogram in coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>773-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The computer-analysed 3 orthogonal lead system (&quot;3-lead ECG&quot;) provides a rapid and consistent interpretation of the electrocardiogram. In 102 patients undergoing selective coronary arteriography, the ability of such a system to predict the presence of absence of coronary artery disease and the site of myocardial ischaemia was compared with that of the conventional scalar electrocardiogram interpreted by cardiologists (&quot;12-lead ECG&quot;). Each system predicted the site of myocardial ischaemia with equal accuracy. The 3-lead ECG was a more sensitive index (3-lead ECG sensitivity=77%; 12-lead ECG sensitivity=70%) but less specific (3-lead ECG specificity=74%; 12-lead ECG specificity=78%). In coronary artery disease, the predictive &quot;index of merit&quot; for the 3-lead ECG was 0-51, compared with 0-48 for the 12-lead ECG. These results provide further justification for the routine use of the 3 orthogonal lead electrocardiogram.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
